   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend part P of chapter 39 of the laws of 2019, amending the public\nauthorities law relating to the acquisition and disposition of real property,\nin relation to extending the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8852",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8852-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd Part P §2, Chap 39 of 2019",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8852\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend part P of chapter 39 of the laws of 2019, amending the\npublic authorities law relating to the acquisition and disposition of\nreal property, in relation to extending the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would extend the 2019 provisions of Section § 1267 of the\nPublic Authorities Law for three years.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill extends the 2019 provisions of § 1267 of the PAL\nfor three years.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOn June 24, 2019 Public Authorities Law § 1267, MTA's condemnation stat-\nute, was amended to specify an interest rate to be paid of six percent,\neffective for three years concluding in June 2022. This rate is the same\nrate paid by municipal corporations, such as the City of New York under\nGeneral Municipal Law § 3-a (2), which provides that \"(the rate of\ninterest to be paid upon any judgement or accrued claim against the\nmunicipal corporation arising out of condemnation proceedings_ shall not\nexceed six per centum.\" Without further action, MTA's rate of interest\nwill revert to the statutory rate of nine per centum pursuant to Uncon-\nsolidated Laws § 2501 (see also CPLR § 5004). Not only does this legis-\nlation give parity to the MTA with other governmental entities, it\nremoves incentives to intentionally delay MTA capital projects in order\nto earn greater amounts of interests by unnecessarily lengthening\ncondemnation proceedings.\n\nThe change in interest rate has resulted in reduced costs with respect\nto condemnation claims for additional compensation, saving the MTA over\n$1 million since 2019, including several hundred thousand dollars on the\nfinal two remaining Second Avenue Subway, Phase 1 matters. However, due\nto Covid-19 and the resulting pause of MTA's Capital Program, important\nprojects the 2019 legislation was intended to benefit have been delayed,\nincluding Phase 2 of the Second Avenue Subway and the Penn Access\nProject. With the capital program restarted and with several significant\nprojects to commence, such as the reconstruction of the Jamaica Bus\nDepot, Penn Station Access, and Second Avenue Subway Phase II, it is\ncritical that the six per cent interest rate is maintained.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
